<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kul-Oba</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">4th century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kul-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oba-dynasty">Oba</a> is a Scythian burial mound near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerch">Kerch</a> in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimea">Crimea</a>, raised in the fourth century BCE. Excavated in 1830, it contained the stone tomb of a nobleman with his wife and a servant, together with rich gold and electrum objects, among them a vase showing Scythian warriors. The finds, now in the Hermitage Museum, show Greek craftsmen of the Bosporus working in Scythian taste.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kul-oba.jpg" alt="Kul-Oba" width="320" height="356" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Joanbanjo · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vas_d%27or_amb_representaci%C3%B3_d%27escites,_kurgan_de_Kul-Oba,_segona_meitat_del_segle_IV_aC.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
